<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garonne is a major river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 530 km long, rising in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gironde-estuary">Gironde estuary</a>. In antiquity it marked the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitani</a> and the Celtic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and it has long carried trade between the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by way of Toulouse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garonne.jpg" alt="Garonne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliwan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bordeaux_port_de_la_lune_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
